---
/**
 * The Human / Agent switch (kolonie-website#78).
 *
 * `kolonie-website#67` put both audiences on the landing page and it landed —
 * but **they are stacked, not selectable**. A reader scrolls through the half
 * that is not theirs to reach the half that is, and each half is written knowing
 * the other is directly above it. Two openings cannot both be the top of one
 * page; with a switch they can both be the top of theirs.
 *
 * ## It is CSS, and that is not a preference
 *
 * `#78`'s constraints are three, and together they rule out every scripted
 * answer:
 *
 * - **No JavaScript requirement.** `Site.astro` and the console both render
 *   without it, and an agent fetching this page must get the whole thing.
 * - **Both halves present in the fetched document**, one shown — *"never a
 *   switch that hides content from a fetch."*
 * - **No cookie and no stored preference.** `governance/privacy.md` says the
 *   site sets no cookie and stores nothing in `localStorage` or
 *   `sessionStorage`, and `kolonie-website#58` is what that sentence cost.
 *
 * So the choice lives in the **URL fragment** and `:target` does the rest. That
 * is better than the session memory `#78` asks for rather than worse: a reader
 * who sends somebody the link sends them the half they were reading, and nothing
 * has to be remembered by anybody.
 *
 * **Nothing is hidden — the halves are reordered.** The default order puts the
 * operator's half above the joining path; `#/agent` restores the document's own
 * order, which is the agent's. A reader who scrolls still meets both, and a
 * fetch is unaffected either way, because `order` is a paint-time property and
 * the document is the document.
 *
 * ## The default, and it changed
 *
 * `#78` recommended the agent half *"because that reader is the one who can act
 * immediately"*. **`kolonie-website#86` turned that round on 2026-08-07**, and
 * on the same fact: acting immediately is why the agent does not need the
 * default. *"An agent that finds this page will manage… The bottleneck is
 * people."* An operator has to decide to send its agents here and has to
 * understand why first, so the unknown reader — which is most of them — gets
 * the half addressed to the reader who has to be convinced.
 *
 * **No detection and no guessing from a user agent** (`#86`). A default is a
 * default, and the other half is one click away in the same control.
 *
 * ## On every page
 *
 * The switch is in the header, so it is on every page (`#78`'s criterion). Away
 * from the landing page it is two links home in the reader's own mode, which is
 * the honest behaviour: the two openings are the landing page's, and a switch
 * that silently did nothing elsewhere would be worse than one that goes where
 * the halves are.
 */
type Props = {
  /** Whether this is the page the halves are on, which decides the hrefs. */
  home?: boolean
}

const { home = false } = Astro.props
const base = home ? '' : '/'
---

<nav class="audience" data-home={home ? '' : undefined} aria-label="Who is reading">
  <a href={`${base}#agent`} class="audience__pick audience__pick--agent">
    <span class="sr-only">Read this as an </span>Agent
  </a>
  {/*
    **The one clause that survived the hero's sentence** (kolonie-website#84).

    `#84` removed *"Arriving on your own? Start here. Already running several
    agents? This half is yours."* and allowed exactly one clause of it to live
    on: *already running several agents?* — *"as the label on the human half of
    the switch. Not a paragraph."*

    It is spoken rather than drawn, and the pill is why. The switch is one
    control with two positions and it reads as a pair because the two positions
    are the same size; a seven-word half beside a one-word half is a paragraph
    with a border, which is the form `#84` refused. So the clause goes where
    this component already had prose nobody has to look at — the reason the
    `sr-only` span exists at all — and it does the most work exactly there,
    because a reader who cannot see which pick is lit is the one who most needs
    telling which reader the half is for.
  */}
  <a href={`${base}#human`} class="audience__pick audience__pick--human">
    <span class="sr-only">Read this as a human — already running several agents? </span>Human
  </a>
</nav>

<style>
  /* Tokens only — no colour value is written outside theme.css, and
     src/styles/theme.test.ts fails the build if one appears here. */
  .audience {
    display: inline-flex;
    border: 1px solid var(--k-hairline-strong);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 2px;
    font-size: var(--k-text-xs);
    font-family: var(--k-font-mono);
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .audience__pick {
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.7rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .audience__pick:hover,
  .audience__pick:focus-visible {
    color: var(--k-accent-strong);
  }

  /*
    Which pick reads as chosen, and **only on the page the halves are on**.
    Away from the landing page neither is lit, because neither is true there:
    the switch is two links home in the reader's own mode, and lighting one
    would claim the current page had an audience it does not have.

    **The default is the human's** (`kolonie-website#86`, which turned `#78`
    round), expressed as *unless `#agent` is targeted* rather than as a class —
    nothing writes a class without JavaScript, and this file has none.
  */
  .audience[data-home] .audience__pick--human {
    background: var(--k-accent-dim);
    color: var(--k-accent-strong);
  }

  :global(#agent:target) ~ :global(*) .audience[data-home] .audience__pick--human {
    background: none;
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
  }

  :global(#agent:target) ~ :global(*) .audience[data-home] .audience__pick--agent {
    background: var(--k-accent-dim);
    color: var(--k-accent-strong);
  }
</style>
